Factoring"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124261} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565432}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124558 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124367} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050225}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565463}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125731 :tutorial/content "We can extend our knowledge of limits to multivariable functions. We'll first consider the two-variable case. \n\nWe denote $\\mathbf{x}=(x,y)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_0 = (a,b)$ as a particular, fixed vector. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined in some neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ \n\nThen, the notation\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f (x,y) = L\n$$\n\nmeans that the value of $f$ can be made as close as we want to $L,$ provided that $\\mathbf x$ is sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{x}_0.$\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- This definition of a limit also extends to functions of more than two variables in the natural way.\n\n- All of the rules set out by the algebra of limits are valid for functions of several variables.\n\nWe say that $f(x,y)$ is **continuous** at $(a,b)$ if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (a,b)} f (x,y) = f(a,b).\n$$\n\nSo, if a function is continuous at $(a,b),$ then the limit can be evaluated by simply plugging $(x,y) = (a,b)$ into the function.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nThis function is continuous for all $(x,y)$ with the exception of $(0,0)$ where the denominator vanishes. Since the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(x,y) = (2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eccfdda1-288a-5169-adde-a00163fa348f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2480 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125732 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a function of a single variable $f(x),$ if the left and right limits both exist and are equal at some point $x=a$, then this is sufficient to conclude that the limit exists at $x=a.$ \n\nSo, if we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x\\to a^+} f(x) = L,\n$$\n\nthen we may conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} f(x) = L.\n$$\n\nFor a multivariable function, the situation is not so straightforward. For a function of two variables $f(x,y),$ we may only conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f(x,y) = L,\n$$\n\nif we can show that the limit is the same *for all possible paths* on which $\\mathbf x$ can approach $\\mathbf{x}_0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4b46a0a-5b1c-58eb-aa32-dd0ccd57f7a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2482 :tutorial/title "The Definition of a Limit: Single Variable Functions vs. Multivariable Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125754 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we may need to simplify a limit before we can evaluate it. \n\nFor example, let's consider the limit\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2}.\n$$\n\nDirect substitution causes this limit to take the indeterminate form $\\dfrac 00$ because the numerator and denominator both evaluate to zero at the given point. \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = \\dfrac{(2)^2 + 2(2)(-1)}{(2)(-1) + 2(-1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by factoring the numerator and denominator and then canceling a common factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy}{xy+2{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{y(x+2y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = -2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8f5c0d1f-e38c-5cd7-97e2-1aaaac5158b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2883 :tutorial/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125809 :tutorial/content "However, it is often easy to show that a limit does not exist for a multivariable function. All we have to do is find two paths on which the limit evaluates to different results. \n\nAs an example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)} \\dfrac{y}{x+y}\n$$\n\nClearly, direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00.$ Let's compute the limit along the paths $y=x$ and $y=x^2.$ We will see that the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{y}{x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{x+x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{1+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{y}{x +y} = \\text{DNE}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8040690e-6d4c-50ac-8b9f-f92615717816" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3960 :tutorial/title "Proving That a Limit Does Not Exist by Evaluating It Along Different Paths"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147498 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147213} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df3a798c-f226-5b4f-8d65-4a305de2b087" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645758}] :knowledge-point/title "Checking ODEs for Exactness"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147499 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147210} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049789}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645746}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147500 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147211} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cbd51b4-cbd1-5e18-8c49-44be7967cd38" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050869}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186665388}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exact Differential Equation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147501 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147214} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bb08d6e-ae86-50f5-8cb4-d7fe50595849" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186665396}] :knowledge-point/title "Rearranging an ODE Into Exact Form and Then Solving"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148644 :tutorial/content "Recall from multivariable calculus that if we have a function $u(x,y),$ then the **exact derivative** of $u$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u = \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\textrm d x + \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nAn **exact differential equation** is a differential equation whose left-hand side is the exact derivative of some function. That is, for some function $u(x,y),$ the equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u = 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, for an exact differential equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\textrm d x + \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\textrm d y = 0.\n$$\n\nSometimes, we might be given a differential equation written in **differential form.**\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm d x + Q(x,y)\\textrm d y = 0\n$$\n\nNote that this is equivalent (for $Q\\neq 0$) to the equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = -\\dfrac{P(x,y)}{Q(x,y)}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether an equation written in differential form is exact, we can use the **exactness test**, which checks whether $P$ and $Q$ could come from the same underlying function $u(x,y).$ Our differential equation is exact if and only if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\,.\n$$\n\nWe'll justify the exactness test soon, and we'll also learn how to solve these types of equations. But for now, let's get some practice at using the exactness test to determine which differential equations are exact." :tutorial/id #uuid "702513d5-4b0f-537b-bce1-fcd03bdd9736" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14035 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148645 :tutorial/content "Remember that if a differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm d x + Q(x,y)\\textrm d y = 0\n$$\n\nis *exact*, then the left-hand side is the total derivative of some function $u = u(x,y).$ Thus, we can write this equation as\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u = 0.\n$$\n\nThis condition means that $u(x,y)$ does not change as we move along a solution curve — its value stays constant. Therefore, solutions trace out the level curves\n\n$$\nu(x,y) = c\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThus, to find $u(x,y),$ we look for a function whose partial derivatives satisfy\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y), \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y).\n$$\n\nSolving an exact equation consists of finding $u(x,y)$ and then setting $u(x,y) = c.$\n\nWe will see how to perform this step by step in the next example." :tutorial/id #uuid "902fd68e-d0df-5df3-803d-94238498c20a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14036 :tutorial/title "General Solutions of Exact ODEs"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148646 :tutorial/content "So far in this lesson, we have been using the exactness test, which states that a differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm d x + Q(x,y)\\textrm d y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if and only if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\,.\n$$\n\nNow, let's explain why this test works.\n\nThe reason this test works lies in the very definition of an exact equation. An exact differential equation must come from a single function $u(x,y).$ The equation itself is simply the total differential of this function set to zero\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\textrm d x + \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\textrm d y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $u = u(x,y) = c$ is the solution.\n\nBy comparing the terms of our original ODE with the total differential, we immediately see how $P$ and $Q$ must be defined in terms of $u:$\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad Q = \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nTo derive the exactness condition, we take the partial derivative of $P$ with respect to $y$ and the partial derivative of $Q$ with respect to $x$. This requires us to look at the second-order partial derivatives of the function $u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}u}{∂y∂x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}u}{∂x∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the exactness test condition is equivalent to the equality of the mixed second partial derivatives of the function $u.$\n\nNotice that we assumed the equality of the mixed partials, meaning that $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial y\\partial x}=\\dfrac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial x\\partial y}.\n$$ This requires that $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial y\\partial x}$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial x\\partial y}$ both exist and are continuous, which is true for almost all of the functions that we'll come across." :tutorial/id #uuid "0f8f8bf3-1139-5718-b279-d4ee578451eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14037 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Exactness Test"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172038 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171844} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e7e0a0-9388-5346-ab96-f2dec237c9f5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048822}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670698} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670691}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172039 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171845} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd092f29-08bf-5425-88a4-8cba4f0a31c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048480}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672645} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672643}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172040 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171846} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2334101-5266-5853-aedf-04c85746b5cc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048179}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693308} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186690772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186690773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186690781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693326}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172041 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171847} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75710e99-e13c-54ba-9401-486bfec15284" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048795}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725038} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725095}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rectangular Regions Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173161 :tutorial/content "Let's plot the slope field of the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}=x-y.\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side of our equation is a function of both $x$ and $y.$ However, the procedure for sketching the slope field is the same as usual.\n\nWe start by setting up a table of values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x=-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x=1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y=-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe then calculate the slope at each point and record each answer in the table. For example, the slope at the point $(0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right|_{(0,1)} = 0-1 ={\\color{blue}{-1}}.\n$$\n\nFilling in our table gives the following set of results:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\t&-1 \t&0 \t&1 \\\\\n\\hline\n -1\t&0 \t&1 \t&2\\\\\n\\hline\n0\t&-1 \t&0 \t&1\\\\\n\\hline\n1\t&-2 \t&{\\color{blue}{-1}} \t&0\\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x=-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x=1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}{-1}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y=-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, sketching our slope field, we get the following diagram:\n\n![](images/2a/2a86de3bdb5716e2be58f02124ccfd7470c9d8eda50abdb01052200e9fecfa0d.png)\n\nWe're done!\n\nNote that if we were to pick more points and use a computer to plot our slope field, it would look more like this:\n\n![](images/42/42508880923b52e9a63b90514c01b33b68c45385cb1650e44832953a6111ee7b.png)\n\nFrom here, it's easy to get an idea of the solution curves. For example, three particular solutions to the differential equation are given below.\n\n![](images/76/7634837eabf2be5ceb2ac56d22576170238b2640181aadde2417e8ff7a2bbd83.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "790a4898-1668-5214-a911-83977b559c34" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5050 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
